+
+# Throttle login submissions per client IP
+
+## Context
+
+`POST /login` accepted an unlimited number of submissions. A credential
+stuffing script could try thousands of passwords a minute against one
+account and the app answered each one the same way it answers a typo —
+nothing slowed an attacker down.
+
+## Decision
+
+The login submission is throttled at **5 requests per minute per client
+IP**, using the framework's `createRateLimitMiddleware()` wired as an
+inline middleware on the `login.store` route only
+([app/Http/Middleware/LoginThrottle.ts](../../app/Http/Middleware/LoginThrottle.ts)).
+
+- Every submission counts, valid credentials included, so a successful
+  login cannot reset the counter — the check runs ahead of body
+  validation, which also makes a malformed payload just as expensive.
+- Over the limit the request is refused with **429** and a `Retry-After`
+  header (seconds), set on the response itself rather than through the
+  context's prepared headers so it survives however the response is
+  built.
+- Client identity is the connection's peer address (`server.requestIP()`
+  via the limiter's default key generator). `trustProxy` stays **off**:
+  this app is not deployed behind a proxy that strips them, so
+  `X-Forwarded-For` and friends are attacker-controlled and each forged
+  value would buy a fresh bucket. When no address can be resolved the
+  generator falls back to a single shared bucket for the route — those
+  requests are throttled together rather than let through.
+- Counters live in memory. This is a single-process app and there is
+  nothing worth persisting across a restart. The store is created per
+  route registration, so each application instance (a test app included)
+  starts clean.
+
+Only the submission is limited. `GET /login`, registration, and every
+other route are untouched — a throttled client can still load the form
+and read the error.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Online password guessing against one account is capped at 5 tries a
+  minute, and the attempts are cheap to refuse.
+- A shared egress IP (office NAT, mobile carrier) shares one bucket, so
+  several people signing in at once from one address can be throttled.
+  5/minute leaves room for ordinary retries; raise `LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS`
+  if a real deployment shows otherwise.
+- Deploying behind a reverse proxy will need this revisited — the peer
+  address becomes the proxy's, collapsing every client into one bucket.
+  That is the point at which `trustProxy` (with a proxy that strips the
+  headers) becomes the right setting.
+- Multi-process or multi-instance deployment needs a shared store
+  (Redis) — the in-memory counters are per process.
